[/asy]. Let's first note that $A$, $X$, and $Y$ form a right triangle, and that the circle with diameter $XY$ is also a right triangle with center $O$.  Note that $\triangle ABX$ and $\triangle ACY$ are copies of one another by a $180^\circ$ rotation around $X$. Thus, since the points diametrically opposite $X$ and $Y$ lie on $BC$, the circle is tangent to $BC$ as well.  Thus, $A$, $X$, $Y$, the center of its incircle lie on a circle!  Hence, $A$ is the center of the incircle.  It follows that the radius $r$ of the incircle of $\triangle ABC$ has $r = 3$, since it must be the inradius.  The area of the portion of the circle outside the triangle is equal to the area of the semicircle we have drawn minus the area of $\triangle AXO$.\begin{align*} \text{Area}& = \frac{1}{2}\pi(r^2) - \frac{1}{2}r(2r) \\& = \frac{1}{2}\pi(3^2) - \frac{1}{2}(3)(2\times 3) \\& = \boxed{\frac{9}{2}\left(\pi -2\right)}\end{align*} .
